<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karimnagar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karimnagar is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Manair river, a tributary of the Godavari. Its region was ruled in the 8th to 10th centuries by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vemulawada">Vemulavada</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, feudatories of the Rashtrakutas, and later by the Kakatiyas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-shahi-dynasty">Qutb Shahis</a> and the Nizams of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. The city is known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> filigree work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karimnagar.jpg" alt="Karimnagar" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Naveen Gujje · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teen_minar_Elgandal_fort_Karimnagar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
